 "summary": "# Security Analysis Report: `jsonschema-4.25.1`\n\n## Package Overview\n`jsonschema` is a widely used Python library and CLI tool implementing JSON Schema validation. Its core runtime codebase consists of schema validation logic (`validators.py`, `_keywords.py`, `_legacy_keywords.py`), format checkers (`_format.py`), type checkers (`_types.py`), utilities (`_utils.py`), exceptions (`exceptions.py`), and a command-line interface (`cli.py`).\n\n---\n\n## Key Security Findings\n\n### 1. Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) and Local File Probing via Default `$ref` Resolution (Medium / Context-Dependent High)\n- **CWEs**: CWE-918 (Server-Side Request Forgery), CWE-73 (External Control of File Name or Path)\n- **Locations**:\n  - `jsonschema/validators.py` (lines 109–115, 132–134): `_warn_for_remote_retrieve` and `_DEFAULT_REGISTRY`\n  - `jsonschema/validators.py` (lines 1183–1228): `RefResolver.resolve_remote`\n- **Analysis**:\n  - By default, `jsonschema` validators instantiate `_DEFAULT_REGISTRY = Registry(retrieve=_warn_for_remote_retrieve)` when an explicit `registry` argument is not provided.\n  - Despite emitting a deprecation warning discouraging automatic network retrieval, `_warn_for_remote_retrieve(uri)` still constructs a `urllib.request.Request` and invokes `urllib.request.urlopen(request)` on any unresolved `$ref` URI.\n  - Similarly, the legacy `RefResolver.resolve_remote(uri)` invokes `requests.get(uri)` (for `http`/`https` when `requests` is installed) or falls back to `urllib.request.urlopen(uri)` for all other schemes.\n  - Because `urllib.request.urlopen` supports `http://`, `https://`, `ftp://`, and `file://` URL schemes out of the box, validating against an **untrusted or user-controlled JSON Schema** allows an attacker to trigger arbitrary outbound HTTP/FTP requests to internal services (SSRF) or read local JSON files via `file://` URIs.\n- **Remediation**:\n  - Applications processing untrusted schemas must explicitly pass an empty or strictly allowlisted `referencing.Registry()` (with no default network `retrieve` callable) to validator classes/functions.\n  - Library maintainers should complete the deprecation cycle and disable `_warn_for_remote_retrieve` by default in future releases, or restrict `urlopen` schemes to prevent `file://` and `ftp://` access when remote retrieval is used.\n\n### 2. Regular Expression Denial of Service (ReDoS) in Schema Pattern Matching (Medium / Context-Dependent)\n- **CWEs**: CWE-1333 (Inefficient Regular Expression Complexity), CWE-400 (Uncontrolled Resource Consumption)\n- **Locations**:\n  - `jsonschema/_keywords.py` (lines 22, 218): `patternProperties` and `pattern` validators\n  - `jsonschema/_legacy_keywords.py` (line 383)\n  - `jsonschema/_format.py` (lines 18, 419): `is_regex` format checker and format validation regexes\n- **Analysis**:\n  - `jsonschema` uses Python's standard `re` module (`re.search` / `re.compile`), which employs a backtracking NFA engine without execution time bounds.\n  - When untrusted schemas are evaluated against inputs (via `pattern` or `patternProperties`), catastrophic backtracking regular expressions can cause severe CPU exhaustion and Denial of Service.\n- **Remediation**:\n  - Do not evaluate untrusted schemas without restricting or pre-validating regular expression complexity (e.g., using linear-time regex engines like `google-re2` / `re2` where ECMA-262 compatibility permits, or enforcing schema sanitization).\n\n---\n\n## Audited Areas Without Vulnerabilities\n- **Code Execution & Deserialization (CWE-94, CWE-502)**: No unsafe deserialization (`pickle`, `yaml.load`, `marshal`) or dynamic code evaluation (`eval`, `exec`) is present in the library. JSON parsing uses standard `json.loads`. Dynamic module loading in `cli.py` (via `pkgutil.resolve_name`) is strictly limited to explicit CLI arguments (such as `--validator`).\n- **OS Command Injection (CWE-78)**: Runtime package modules do not invoke `subprocess`, `os.system`, or shell commands (`noxfile.py` is development-only).",
